        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "A few years ago, I saw an exhibition of Pablo Picasso’s "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "sculptures",
            "zh": "雕塑作品"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Maybe, because he thought of himself as a painter first, he was freer to play with the art of sculpture. For example, what can you do with a pair of bicycle "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "handlebars",
            "zh": "把手"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "saddle",
            "zh": "车座"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "? The Spanish artist turned them into a “"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "bull",
            "zh": "公牛"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "”. The exhibition brought to mind Picasso’s famous "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "quote",
            "zh": "引语"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", “Every child is an artist. The problem is how to remain an artist once we grow up.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "I’m a writer and "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "illustrator",
            "zh": "插图画家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of children’s books. Sometimes, I go into schools and read my stories to children. I also draw with them. I show them how to draw the "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "characters",
            "ref": "v-character"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in my books. These children draw after me. Their drawings, however, always make mine seem lifeless. I’m always surprised by them. Children just put their ideas out there. They do not overthink. Adults worry about how other people judge them. They often think a lot before putting pen to paper."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Recently, I’ve been working with a primary school. I see six-year-olds "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "expressing",
            "ref": "v-express"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " themselves freely through their drawings. However, when they leave at the age of eleven, many of them feel uncomfortable about the act of drawing. They have learned that there is a “right” way to draw, and a “wrong” way. There isn’t. The most important thing is to draw and to express yourself through your work."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Picasso painted and sculpted like a child. He expressed himself freely. He cared little about what other people thought of him. Perhaps this is why his paintings and sculptures are still popular today."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
